00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:33.340
Expanded use of multi-trial aggregation and position randomization in public leaderboards will

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:40.900
follow the LLM as judge reliability findings. Okay, let's pop the hood on the LLM as a judge findings

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:47.560
because the variance numbers matter for anyone running eVALs. Everyone treats LLM as a judge as a

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:53.620
reliable low-cost replacement for human Raiders. In practice, it is a noisy measurement process

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:59.520
whose variance depends on prompt phrasing, position, and temperature in ways that single trial

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:05.800
scores hide. The core issue is that pairwise preference and point-wise scoring are only loosely

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:11.880
coupled. Judges often declare a winner even when their own scalar scores show negligible difference.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:17.840
Temperature and prompt paraphrases shift majority outcomes in roughly a quarter of cases.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:24.920
First position bias can reach 72 percent for some models. Cross-judge agreement between two

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:32.800
open AI models sits at 76 percent with moderate Kappa. The practical fix is to run 11 to 15 repeated

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:39.380
trials with randomized order and report both the majority vote and the flip rate. Anything less leaves

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:45.760
high stakes rankings vulnerable to sampling noise rather than true capability differences. So when should

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:51.640
you actually reach for this versus the alternative? Run the multi-trial protocol whenever the ranking

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:58.180
will drive model selection or paper claims. Tomorrow, keep an eye on the MIT Open Wates release for

